{#-
  Deliberately empty override of the theme's `sitemap.xml` template.

  MkDocs registers `sitemap.xml` as a static template and skips writing any
  static template whose rendered output is blank, logging
  `Template skipped: 'sitemap.xml' generated empty output.` Because this file
  contains nothing but this Jinja comment, it renders to the empty string, so
  the build writes neither `site/sitemap.xml` nor `site/sitemap.xml.gz`.

  WHY THE SITEMAP IS SUPPRESSED RATHER THAN POPULATED
  --------------------------------------------------
  The theme's sitemap template enumerates `page.canonical_url`, which the
  generator resolves only once a canonical site address is configured. With no
  such address the loop yields nothing, so the build published a well-formed
  file containing an empty `<urlset>` — a sitemap advertising zero URLs for
  four live pages. That is worse than no sitemap: a crawler reads it as an
  authoritative statement that the site has no indexable pages.

  Populating it would require declaring an absolute origin, and no truthful one
  exists to declare. This documentation is published by Backstage TechDocs
  under a portal base address that this repository cannot know; the repository
  itself serves no site (GitHub Pages is disabled), and the source-hosting URL
  is not where the pages are served from. Inventing an origin would put a false
  `<link rel="canonical">` on all four pages and false `<loc>` entries here —
  precisely the class of unevidenced claim this remediation exists to remove.
  The site configuration is additionally forbidden from carrying that key.

  Suppression is therefore the honest outcome, and it costs nothing in this
  deployment: the portal proxies this output and builds its own search index
  rather than crawling a sitemap.

  Removing this file restores the generator's default template and, with it,
  the empty `<urlset>`. Do not delete it without first configuring a canonical
  address that is genuinely true of where the site is served.
-#}
